SNAPSHOT TESTS
==============

Run the compiler on a project and keep its output as well as some intermediate state in files,
so that a change to the compiler shows up as a diff.
Think of them as a _contrast agent_ that alerts us to changes.


DESCRIPTION
-----------

A project's snapshots live beside it, under its urt directory: next to
`hello.urp` is `hello.urt`, and the snapshots are `hello.urt/snapshot`.  Each
case there is a run of the compiler, with the flags it was given and what it
wrote:

  hello.ur
  hello.urp
  hello.urt/
  `-- snapshot/
      `-- 1/                      a case; named, by a number unless told otherwise
          |-- args                extra flags, if any; the project is implied
          `-- expected/
              |-- exitcode        the compiler's exit status
              |-- stdout.txt      what the compiler wrote to stdout
              |-- stderr.txt      ... and to stderr
              |-- settings.txt    -saveSettings   the parsed and merged job
              |-- parsetree.ur    -saveParsetree  the program as parsed
              |-- typecheck.urs   -saveTypecheck  the signatures inferred
              |-- schema.sql      -sql            the database schema
              |-- client.js       -saveJs         the client-side script
              |-- endpoints.json  -endpoints      the URLs the app serves
              `-- server.c        -saveC          the generated C

One project can have several cases (the same program under three database
backends, say), each with its own args and the artifacts it tracks:

  shop.urt/
  `-- snapshot/
      |-- mysql/
      |   |-- args                -dbms mysql
      |   `-- expected/
      |       `-- schema.sql
      |-- postgres/
      |   `-- ...
      `-- sqlite/
          `-- ...

A case can go on from the compiler to the application it compiles: under
request-response/ are runs, each the application started afresh and sent
requests, one after the other in the order of their names:

  shop.urt/
  `-- snapshot/
      `-- sqlite/
          |-- args                        -dbms sqlite
          |-- expected/
          |-- app.exe                     the application as last built; not for git
          `-- request-response/
              `-- checkout/               a run; named like a case
                  |-- args                extra flags for the application, if any
                  |-- secret              the key it signs with: 16 bytes or more
                  |-- db.sql              the database it starts from, if it uses one
                  |-- interaction/
                  |   |-- 01-add.request  what to send
                  |   |-- 01-add.response what came back, as `curl -i` prints it
                  |   |-- 01-add.sql      what that changed in the database, if anything
                  |   |-- 02-pay.request
                  |   `-- 02-pay.response
                  `-- out/
                      |-- stdout          what the server printed
                      |-- stderr
                      `-- db_final.sql    the database it left behind

A request is a method, a path, and then fields (-d) and cookies (-b) in curl's
spelling, quoted as for a shell; nothing else is taken:

  POST /Shop/add
      -d 'name=kettle'
      -d 'price=12'
      -b 'visitor=somebody'

Runs share nothing.  Each has its own database -- SQLite, so the case's args
need -dbms sqlite -- loaded from db.sql, which holds the schema and any rows
to start with; within a run, the requests build on each other.  So that the
same requests get the same answers, the application's clock is pinned (the
first request is served at 2000-01-01 00:00:00 UTC, each further one a second
later, and the database agrees), as are its random numbers and its time zone,
UTC; its heap starts at 256 MiB, so that no handler is run a second time
for the heap to grow; every request is for the host 'localhost', from the
user agent 'urt'.

A case with runs is compiled all the way, so its args cannot hold -stop,
-stopQuiet or -tc, and it fails if that does not give an application.  The
compiler builds it where the project has it built, and from there it is moved
into the case as app.exe, where it stays, to be started by hand if need be.
A run's args cannot hold -t: its requests are sent one after the other, and
with more threads than one it is a matter of chance which of them answers.
Nor --, after which the server would ignore the flags urt adds (the address,
the port range, -d3).  Runs need curl and sqlite3 (3.37 or later) on the PATH.

Nothing else may be under snapshot/, bar a README.md beside the cases or in
one, or beside the runs or in one: a file this program would not read is
refused rather than left to rot.
The urt directory itself may hold a README.md, and a 'project' file naming
the project when the directory's name does not; other tools keep their own
directories there.


USAGE
-----

  urt snapshot [PATH...]                     update the snapshots below the given projects
  urt snapshot --whine [PATH...]             fail on a diff (you want this in CI)
  urt snapshot --list [PATH...]              list the cases matching the given path(s)
  urt snapshot --create [PROJECT] [CASE...]  create cases for the given project
  urt snapshot --create-run [PATH] [RUN...]  create runs for the one case at the given path
  urt snapshot --edit [PATH] [ARTIFACT...]   edit the tracked set of a case

PATH is a project (demo/alert[.urp]), its urt directory (demo/alert.urt), its
snapshots (demo/alert.urt/snapshot), one case (demo/alert.urt/snapshot/1) or a
directory to search for snapshots.  It defaults to the current directory.

With '--create', cases are set up for a project, named as given or by the
next number: an empty 'args' file and an 'expected' directory of empty
artifacts each.  On a terminal the artifacts to track are ticked in a
checklist and a run is offered to fill them in; otherwise all of them are
made, to be pruned by hand and filled in by a plain run.  With no PROJECT it
takes the only .urp in the current directory.

With '--create-run', runs are set up for a case, named as given or by the
next number: an empty 'args', a 'secret', the schema as the compiler gives it
for the case as 'db.sql' if the program has tables, and a first request to
replace.  A plain run fills in the rest.

With '--edit', the artifacts a case tracks are ticked in a checklist.  PATH
names the case, or a project or directory holding several, to choose from;
the current directory otherwise.  A newly tracked artifact starts empty, and
a run is offered to fill it in.  Named outright, the ARTIFACTs become the
tracked set without asking, for scripts.

The compiler is the one URWEB names, run with the flags in URWEB_FLAGS if
that is set, or else the in-tree build, bin/urweb under the current
directory or beside this program, run with -boot -noEmacs.  A Nix-packaged
urt has URWEB set to the compiler it was built for.

exit 0
